# Lets the interface's poller read cluster status, and nothing else.
#
# The existing rules for this helper are granted to %sudo - the humans - and the
# comments in them say why an application identity must not ride along on an
# operator's rule: %haproxy-mgmt also holds the worker, and a service account
# must not gain a privilege because somebody else needed one. This is that
# separate decision, with its own rule.
#
# What it grants, exactly: run one helper, with no arguments at all. The helper
# accepts no argv and reads a bounded JSON request on stdin, which is what makes
# an exact rule possible - PRIVILEGE.md forbids wildcards in argument matching,
# and without the no-argument form a rule naming a cluster would need one.
#
# The helper writes nothing, takes no lock, and reads the target from the
# published tree rather than from the caller. So the poller cannot choose what
# the interface is told is current, cannot block a deploy, and cannot reach a
# node with anything but this one question.
#
# NOPASSWD because a service has no terminal to type a password at.
#
# Installed 0440 root:root, and accepted by visudo before it is put in place.

POLLER_USER ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /opt/haproxy-mgmt/current/bin/haproxy-status-helper ""
